components.configDocs:
- `shots[]`: one per `<video>` pair (`s#` is the shot, `z#` is its zoom-blur copy). Each has `start`, `dur`, `focus: [x, y]`, the subject as a 0-1 fraction of the frame the streaks converge on, and an optional `to: [x, y]` to track a moving subject.
- `lines.color`: the streak colour.
- `lines.tint`: a body tint for two-tone streaks with a white core, or `null` for pure white.
- `lines.glow`, `lines.glowOpacity`: the bloom behind the streaks.
- `lines.shade`, `lines.shadeOpacity`: a soft dark halo that keeps the streaks legible on bright sky (`null` for none).
- `lines.cruise`: density before the beat.
- `lines.clear: [rx, ry]`: the clear zone around the subject.
- `lines.bottom`: thins the streaks along the bottom edge.
- `lines.vertical`: bias towards the top and bottom edges.
- `lines.layers.{haze, mid, front, near}`:
  - count `n`, base width range `w`, alpha range `a`, life in frames `life` and length jitter `len`;
  - `travel`: px a streak rushes inward over its life;
  - `detach`: the streak leaves the edge and flies in as a needle;
  - `near` is a defocused corner layer that only exists in the burst.
- `timing`:
  - `in`: the streaks shoot in;
  - `beat`: the slam, so put it on your cut or drop;
  - `kicks[]`: smaller re-hits on later cuts;
  - `out`: the streaks retract off the edges;
  - `end`.
- `footage`: `punch` (zoom on the beat), `shake` (px), `push` (slow zoom per shot) and `zoomBlur` (0 turns off the edge zoom blur).

- A: `platform.name`; `author` {name, handle, time, verified}; `body` (wrap the highlighted phrase in [brackets]);
  `media` (two image paths), `clip` (duration chip on the 2nd image); `counts` {replies, relays, likesFrom, likesTo};
  `colors`; `card` {x, y, w, h}; `timing` {in, hiFrom, hiTo, payoff, out, end}.
- B: `platform` {name, room, online}; `messages` [{from, initial, text: [lines], color, av: [2 colours]}, ... the last
  one with `mine: true`]; `reactions` {from, to}; `colors`; `layout` {left, right, bottom}; `timing` {head, m: [..],
  typing, mine, payoff, out, end}.

components.summary:
The pop-in alert creators drop on a beat. In long-form it's the "Cheat activated" help box top-left. In streams and Shorts it's a donation or sub alert.

- **A, Cheat toast (16:9, long-form):**
  - the GTA help box docked top-left;
  - the cheat code is struck in on keycaps;
  - on the lock-in, a real 3D shield emblem spins and ignites green, and the header rolls to CHEAT ACTIVATED.
- **B, Phone notification (9:16, Shorts):**
  - the creators' tier-coloured donation pill drops in at the top like a phone banner;
  - on the beat the amount drops out of the pill, and a tray slams open beneath it with the amount seated inside as real 3D gold numerals (slam, squash and light on one frame);
  - "TOP DONOR" rolls into the pill's amount slot.
  - The sample footage uses the Shorts layout for landscape gameplay: the 16:9 shot at 1.0x in a full-width window over its own blur.

components.configDocs:
- A:
  - `title`, `handle`;
  - `socials`: a list of `{mark, colors:[top, bottom]}`, where `mark` is one of `play`, `camera`, `bird` or `note`;
  - `slab` (position, size, colours, cap height, tracking);
  - `row` (centre y, tile size, gap);
  - `text` (handle size and y);
  - `colors` (handle face, @ gradient, band, stroke);
  - `timing`.
- B:
  - `tags`: two entries of `{mark, handle, colors, lit, at, litAt}`: `colors` is the tile's lacquer, `lit` the plate's lacquer after the press, `at` / `litAt` the @ colour before / after;
  - `safe` (left, right and top edges);
  - `tile`, `plateH`, `stackGap`, `tagW`, `dock` (the scale the tags settle to after the read);
  - `text` (`max` / `min` auto-fit size, Archivo width and weight);
  - `plate` (enamel colours);
  - `timing` (`payoff` is the cut the press lands on; `echo` delays the second tag; `dock` is when they shrink into the corners).
- If you change A's title to letters other than A-Z, 0-9 or `.,'!?-*`, regenerate `a/media/glyphs.js`:

  ```
  FONT=../assets/fonts/Archivo.ttf AXES='{"wght":900,"wdth":100}' node gen-glyphs.js > glyphs.js
  ```

  This needs fontkit.

- **A:**
  - `day`;
  - `time.from` / `time.to` (H:MM or HH:MM, same digit count; each digit is a drum that rolls forward to `to`);
  - `time.suffix` ("PM", "AM" or "" for 24 h);
  - `place`;
  - `type` (sizes, max ink width, extrusion depth and bevel, block centre);
  - `colors` (white enamel, yellow state metal, caret);
  - `timing` (`type` start, `cps` per letter, colon `blinkOff`, `lock`, the `out` wipe, `end`).
- **B:**
  - `caption`;
  - `clock.from` / `clock.to` (24 h HH:MM; rolls forward and wraps past midnight);
  - `type`, `colors`;
  - `timing` (`roll` start and `rollDur`, `endSpeed` arrival speed before the click, `out`, `end`).

components.summary:
A full-frame 9:16 comparison: a GTA V clip and a GTA VI clip in one vertical frame, titled in cinematic wide type, with small tracked labels ("2013 · LOS SANTOS" / "2026 · LEONIDA"). The type tells the upgrade: "GTA V" is the smaller name in 86% white, "GTA VI" the big one in the Leonida sunset gradient. The demo edit cuts like-for-like pairs from both trailers, and both sides always switch on the same frame. GTA 6 arrives as the hit, and the seam between the eras is the light source. On the payoff a specular glint runs along it and it blooms warm, with an exposure flash, shake and RGB split, the year rolling 2013 → 2026 (blurred while it rolls, so no wrong year is ever readable), then a sheen. Every later cut gets its own smaller beat: the glint re-runs the seam and the place labels swap to name what that pair compares. 1080×1920, 30 fps, 5.9 s.

**Use it for:**
- "GTA 5 vs GTA 6" graphics, physics, NPC, car and map comparisons;
- "13 years later" or "then vs now" beats in compilation shorts;
- any A/B where viewers must know at a glance which half is which game.

Five wanted stars light one by one, 1 → 5, as a clip escalates. Each star lands on a beat. The fifth lands on the big hit, the frame the police SUV's nose lifts off the road. Then the whole flip plays at full speed under the maxed row, on a slow 1.08 push. The hit takes:
- a 2-frame hot-white exposure;
- shake and a punch zoom;
- an RGB split;
- a lens streak across the whole star row;
- hard red/blue police light.

The star fills stay white. The police colour lives only in light: the top-corner washes and the stars' own bloom, which turn red and blue together in 4-frame steps.

A flat oxblood reason badge (witness, 911 call, shots, vehicle, helicopter) joins under each new star. The stars and badges are our own drawing, after the sparse star row and red reason badges in the GTA VI gameplay HUD:
- **Stars:** a 2 px dark keyline, faint facets, a lit top edge and a bloom.
- **Badges:** flat `#8E1620` discs with a 2 px `#3A0A0E` rim and 2.5 px stroke glyphs.

**Use it when** a GTA 6 short escalates: a chase, a "5 stars in 6 seconds" clip, or a compilation where every cut gets wilder. Put each star on a beat (a cut, a crash, gunfire) and the fifth on the biggest one. The HUD corner variant also works as a "real-life GTA" overlay on live-action footage.

components.configDocs:
- `duration`: the composition length, in seconds. If you change it, also change `data-duration` on `#root` and on the `<video>` tags.
- `variants["0" | "1"]`:
  - `orient`: `"vertical"` (handle moves up and down) or `"horizontal"` (handle moves left and right).
  - `chip`: `"card"` (solid ink-navy card with place) or `"glass"` (see-through tinted-glass chip, name over year: rgba(14,14,20,.70) with blur 20px and saturate 1.4).
  - `before` and `after`:
    - `name`, `year`, `place` (`place` only shows on cards);
    - `clip`: the video `src`. Use one held shot that is at least `duration` long;
    - `start`: the offset into the clip, in seconds.
  - `beforeShift`: how many px the GTA 5 footage drifts up (vertical) while GTA 6 wipes in, so its subject sits in the top half. It drifts in on its own gentle ramp (`timing.shiftIn`), holds the full drift through the whole drag (a pure wipe), and drifts back as the labels leave (`timing.shiftOut`). Keep it at or below `1920 - seam.showBefore`. Use `0` for none.
  - `afterTop` (vertical only): where the GTA 6 footage box starts, in px from the top. The box runs to the bottom of the frame, so bake that clip at 1080 × (1920 − `afterTop`). Keep it at or below `seam.showAfter`. Use `0` for full frame.
  - `seam`: the seam positions in px (y for vertical, x for horizontal):
    - `home`: the 50/50 resting position;
    - `showBefore`: GTA 5 nearly fills the frame;
    - `showAfter`: GTA 6 nearly fills the frame;
    - `off`: the off-frame start and end position;
    - `knob`: the handle's fixed position on the other axis.
  - `gap`: how far each label stays from the seam (40).
  - `safe`: `[lo, hi]`, the safe-zone span the labels must stay inside along the slider axis (`[230, 1480]` vertical, `[72, 936]` horizontal).
  - `chipX` (vertical: the cards' left edge) and `chipTop` (horizontal: the chips' top edge).
- `timing`:
  - `seamIn` and `seamInDur`: the wipe-in;
  - `chipA` and `chipB`: when the labels land;
  - `drag`: the legs of the drag, each `{ at, dur, to, ease }`, where `to` is a `seam` key. The handle presses in just before the first leg and releases as the last one lands;
  - `labelFade`: how long a label takes to fade when it runs out of room (0.15 s);
  - `shiftIn` and `shiftInDur` (0.08 s, 0.90 s, `power2.out`), `shiftOut` and `shiftOutDur` (4.25 s, 0.55 s, `sine.inOut`): when the `beforeShift` drift runs in and back out. The footage moves at most about 50 px per frame. Finish `shiftOut` before the seam leaves the frame;
  - `out`: when the labels leave;
  - `retract` and `retractDur`: the seam leaving.

```js
CONFIG.variants["0" | "1"] = {
  kicker: "Top 5 · GTA 6",          // small tracked caps above the title
  title:  "Best trailer moments",   // Barlow Condensed 800; auto-fits to 2 lines inside 864 px
  items: [                          // exactly 5. items[0] is chosen first, items[1] second
    { rank: 5, name: "Leonida's finest", sub: "Florida Man energy",
      clip: "assets/plates/p05-truck-yard-h.mp4", from: 0, focusX: 0.5 },   // from = seconds into the file
    { rank: 4, name: "Jason & Lucia", sub: "Golden hour",
      clip: "assets/plates/p06-lucia-gate-h.mp4", from: 0.95, focusX: 0.70 },
    ...
  ]
}
CONFIG.timing[v] = { enter, enterDur, land, lab0, out0, next, nextDur, land1, lab1, out1, dolly, dollyDur, end }
CONFIG.look = { dim: 0.75, back: 0.28, deck: [0.78, 0.62, 0.48, 0.36], backdrop: 0.18, radius: 28 }
// Ring: unchosen-card and card-back brightness; Stack: brightness of the 1st..4th lip behind the front card;
// backdrop brightness (before the ink grade and radial ink field); corner radius (px at the front position)
```

- `rank`: the 200 px sunset numeral on the label. The cards carry no rank chips, so the rank is stated once.
- `name` / `sub`: the label for `items[0]` and `items[1]`. Name the moment the way you would in the video ("Leonida's finest", "The chase"), not the shot. The other three show only as clips, but give them real names too. For Stack flip, pick a first clip that looks clearly different from the second (the sample goes from a daylight street chase to a car interior), so the flip reads as a change and not as a dissolve.
- `focusX` (0–1, default 0.5): only used on `items[1]`, the clip the camera pushes into. It is the horizontal point of the 16:9 clip that the full-frame 9:16 ending centres on (0 = left edge, 1 = right edge). It is clamped to about 0.15–0.85 so the frame stays inside the clip. Put it on the subject's head, including the back of the head and any cap: 0.70 for Jason under the shelter (p06), 0.80 for Jason at the wheel (p03).
- Stack timing: `next` is 3.561 so the flip's edge-on moment lands exactly on a frame. Near edge-on the card drops under the deck and fades out there, so no frame shows it as a sliver and no frame shows one clip through another: wherever the flipping card is over the deck it is fully opaque. If you move `next`, this still holds.
- Timing: Ring fades in as one layer over 0.25 s from `enter` and spins into place until `enter + enterDur`. Stack never fades its cards: they rise in from below the frame, back card first, until `enter + enterDur` (plus a 0.035 s stagger per card). The first card pushes forward at `land`, and its label comes in at `lab0` and leaves at `out0`. The turn (or flip) runs from `next` for `nextDur`. The second card lands at `land1`, its label comes in at `lab1`, the text clears at `out1`, and the push into the clip starts at `dolly`.

components.summary:
Several clips play at once, each with a big number and a short label: the "4 scenes in 4 seconds" format, built native to 9:16. There are no cards or tiles. The frame itself is the grid: full-bleed footage divided by thin black seams, with the numbers sitting on the image where it is calm, and one sunset line of light in the seam as the payoff.

The component is 1080×1920, 30 fps and 5 s long. Every clip plays at 1.0x and is one continuous shot.

**Use it for:**
- "4 details you missed" or "3 scenes in 3 seconds" beats in a compilation short;
- the opening hook of a list video, so the voiceover can say "number 3..." before you cut to each clip full screen;
- recaps where viewers should see several shots side by side.

Present a clip as an in-world social video post, like the vertical feed Rockstar shows inside the GTA VI trailers. The clip sits bright and nearly full-bleed in a rounded card over a blurred copy of itself. The card carries an illustrated emblem avatar, a fictional handle, Rockstar's gold-ringed Follow pill, a caption and stepping counts.

Use it in a GTA 6 short when:
- a clip is "something someone in Leonida posted": Florida-Man moments, police chaos, NPC meltdowns;
- you're doing a "GTA 6 social media" bit;
- you're reframing a clip so it reads as a phone video.

Specs: 1080×1920, 30 fps, 5.5 s. It is a full-frame component, so it always shows footage. Each sample plays one continuous shot with no cuts.

A real, reported GTA 6 news headline set as a press clipping. One phrase in it is swept with the Leonida sunset marker, so the viewer gets the point of the headline in under a second.

It is publication-neutral. There are no outlet names, logos or mastheads. Each card shows only:
- the kind of source ("Local TV report", "Video report");
- the date;
- on the single card, a neutral `NEWS` tag.

The single card runs like a real article:
1. the headline;
2. a meta row;
3. a one-line dek;
4. a 2:1 still lead image;
5. an image credit.

With sample footage, it sits on a soft vignette stage.

**Use it** in a GTA 6 short when you cite the hype or the news around the launch. For example:
- Open a "people are calling in sick for GTA 6" video with the single card.
- Run through what the news is saying with the stack.

It is an overlay: drop it over your own gameplay clip.

components.configDocs:
- **`variants["0"]`**
  - `plate`: the sample footage path.
  - `area`: the zone name, top-left inside the panel.
  - `distance`, `unit`: the remaining distance at the start. It counts down as the arrow drives, rounded up, and never reads 0.0 before ARRIVED.
  - `arrived`: the word shown on arrival.
  - `timing`:
    - `in`: entrance start (y +24 → 0, scale .96 → 1, 0.32 s expo.out, with opacity on power2.out);
    - `blip`;
    - `drawStart`, `drawEnd`: the route draws from the arrow to the blip;
    - `driveStart`, `ramp`: the drive starts and its speed ramps up over `ramp` seconds, then holds constant into contact;
    - `arrive`: the moment of contact, which also fires the dock, the blip pop, the ring, the sweep and the ARRIVED slide;
    - `dock`: how long the arrow takes to slide into the blip (power3.out);
    - `burstDur`, `burstScale`: the arrival ring's length and how far it grows (1 → 2.4 by default);
    - `doneSweep`: how long the white highlight takes to run along the finished route;
    - `settle`: `[start, end]` of the ease that frames the finished trip after arrival;
    - `settleX`: where the blip ends up across the 820 px panel (560 by default);
    - `pulses[]`, `out`, `outDur`.
- **`variants["1"]`**
  - `plate`, `label`, `destination`: the plate path, top label and destination title.
  - `arrived`: the text of the teal pill that pops in beside the title on arrival. `""` hides it. Keep the title short enough to leave room for the pill: "Leonida Keys" plus the pill fills the row.
  - `tag`: an optional chip at the top right, hidden by default (`""`). Set it to `"Route set"` to show it with a 1 px Vice-teal ring.
  - `mapLabels`: `[text, x, y, anchor]` on the 784×500 map. Streets are cut out behind each label automatically.
  - `stats[]`: one entry per column, aligned left / centre / right.
    - `{label, value, decimals, unit, live}` shows its full value from the entrance. With `live: true` it counts down to 0 in step with the arrow, rounded up so it only reads 0 on contact, then steps back to 62% after arrival.
    - `{label, text, unit}` is static and doesn't change on arrival (for example ARRIVE 6:42 PM).
  - **The default trip numbers (24.6 MI / 19 MIN / 6:42 PM) are an example.** Replace them with your own trip before posting.
  - `timing`:
    - `in`: entrance start (y +24 → 0, scale .96 → 1, 0.32 s expo.out, with opacity on power2.out);
    - `blip`, `drawStart`, `drawEnd`, `sweepStart`, `sweepDur` (the pre-drive sweep);
    - `travelStart` to `arrive`: the arrow drives the whole route, with its speed ramping up over `ramp` seconds and then holding constant; `arrive` is the contact;
    - `dock`, `burstDur`, `burstScale`, `doneSweep`: as in v0 (the dock, the ring and the arrival sweep);
    - `push` `[start, end, scale]`;
    - `pulses[]`, `out`, `outDur`.
- **`colors.route`**: the route gradient stops (Leonida sunset core by default).
- **`colors.blip`**: the destination blip fill.
- **`colors.arrived`**: Vice teal, used only for the arrival state. The CSS uses `--teal`.
- **`end`**: the composition length in seconds.

The map geometry lives in `buildCorner()` and `buildCard()`.
- **v0:** the shore control points `SH`, the bay keys (`sliver(...)`), the three `district(...)` block grids, the minor-street fabrics, the three `MAJOR` roads and route `RD`. The water fill is drawn in `#mmWorld`, every stroke in `#mmLines` (clipped to land and faded under the header by `#mmHeadFade`), and the route, arrow and blip in `#mmTop`. All three share one pan transform.
- **v1:** the `COAST` points, `BARRIER` sliver, the city outline, the wetland `WET` shape and marsh pattern, keys path `K`, the island list `[start, end, thickness, side, offset, skew, phase]` and route `RD`. Roads go in the groups clipped to `#gcLand`, so they stop at the coast.

The route is a single SVG path string. Change it to send the GPS somewhere else, and move the `blip(...)` and the `gradient(...)` end to its end point. In v0, keep the route's footprint within about 360×160 so both ends stay in frame.
